src('SRC047','NICAP Nuclear Connection Project directory page on Malmstrom/Echo Flight (Ridge; Sparks comment)','Francis Ridge (coordinator); Brad Sparks quoted','NICAP','updated 2006-02-03','Advocacy directory page','SECONDARY','https://www.nicap.org/babylon/670316_malmstromE.htm','','whole page','%s'%A,'INC019;EVD087;EVD088','Says Boeing, not NSA, investigated and issued a SECRET Report of Engineering Investigation of Echo Flight Incident; claims UFO rumors were denied so the shutdown could be investigated separately.','Advocacy; contains the Sparks interpretation, not evidence.','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED','EXAMINED')
ev('EVD087',"Re-reading SRC001 (Klotz/Salas, 27 Nov 1996, NICAP mirror) against the wing history (SRC002): the 1996 article places Salas as DMCCC in NOVEMBER Flight on the morning of 16 March 1967, with a SECOND, separate shutdown of 'four or five' missiles at November 'at or about the same time' as Echo's ten, and describes UFOs seen at the November LCC and one N LF as well as at Echo LFs. SRC002 says 'No other Wing I configuration lost strategic alert at that time' (0845) and that the only mobile team named checked November's LFs and saw nothing. So the 1996 two-flight same-morning version is contradicted by the one contemporaneous record, and the Oscar relocation (2005 on) is partly the repair of that contradiction. The 1996 article also says early-morning reports came from more than one patrol and maintenance crew (Echo) and the missiles shut down 'over the next half-minute' (matching the crew's 10-40 s estimate, not Figel's 2010 3-4 minutes).",'ANALYST_COMPARISON','SRC001; SRC002','INC019;INC020;EVD077;EVD085','10030','CRITICAL','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD088',"Boeing/Klotz reproduction claim: the 1996 article says the effects were reproduced only by directly introducing a 10-volt pulse onto a data line and that this could be done from outside the shielded system only by an electromagnetic pulse of unknown origin; Sparks (NICAP) says Boeing issued the SECRET engineering report. The wing-history excerpt calls an externally generated signal 'very remote' and says no cause was found; it does not mention a 10 V pulse. Neither the full Engineering Investigation Team report (23 Mar 1967) nor Kaminski's 1 Feb 1997 letter to Klotz has been seen by me; I found a blog repost claiming AARO attributed the event to a 'cascading transformer failure' during an 'electrical storm' - unverified and in conflict with the wing history, which ruled out weather and put the transformer short at ~1450. Wikipedia's mention of a 2025 Pentagon EMP report also remains unsupported.",'ANALYST_ASSESSMENT','SRC001; SRC002; SRC047; search results','INC019;GOV006;EVD085','10099','HIGH','INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE')
